# The wire carries values, not renderings

**DONE.** `nix::Hash`, `nix::ContentAddress` and `nix::Signature` are
their own messages. `PathInfo` carries them as themselves.

## Problem

056 left one question open, and it was the last one about PathInfo's
shape: three of its ten fields crossed the wire as TEXT.

    nar_hash   'sha256:1abc...'
    ca         'fixed:r:sha256:1abc...'
    sigs       ['cache.nixos.org-1:AbCd...']

Each is a rendering of facts glued together, and each made two
demands. A reader who wanted one fact - which algorithm, which key
signed a path, how the bytes were serialised - split a string on
colons to get it. And `_from_parts` on the far side parsed all three
back, with `Hash::parseAnyPrefixed`, `ContentAddress::parse` and
`Signature::parse`, every one of which is a way for a value this repo
had just rendered to fail to come back.

`ca` was the worst of the three: `fixed:r:sha256:<hash>` is a method
AND a hash, and the hash is itself an algorithm and a digest. Three
facts in one string.

## Decided

Carl: *"making them their own messages aligns with my mental model, if
it's not too cumbersome I'd like to go this way."*

It was not cumbersome, and the reason is worth writing down: every
mechanism it needed already existed. A vocabulary crosses as `str`
(047), a wire value nests inside another (`references` is a
`list[StorePath]`), an optional value has presence, and all three C++
types are plain structs with defaulted comparisons.

    message HashMsg { string algorithm = 1; bytes digest = 2; }
    message ContentAddressMsg { string method = 1; HashMsg hash = 2; }
    message SignatureMsg { string key_name = 1; bytes sig = 2; }

`ContentAddress` is the first wire value to HOLD another. That is the
whole point of the shape rather than an incidental consequence.

## The digest crosses as bytes

Not base32, not base16, not SRI. Those are the same 32 bytes in three
alphabets, and choosing one would have put a rendering back on the
wire under a different name. They stay on the class as `@local`
accessors - `str(h)`, `h.base16()`, `h.sri()` - which never leave this
side.

## Hash is constructible, and it is the only one of the three that is

A caller can legitimately HAVE a hash: one read from a flake lock or a
narinfo is two facts they hold, not something a store handed them. So
`Hash('sha256', digest)` works, and refuses a digest of the wrong
length in libstore's own error. `ContentAddress` and `Signature` are
constructible for the same reason one level up.

## What it cost, and what it removed

`decl/pathinfo.py` loses three hatched bodies: an accessor that
RENDERS is a decision, and a member read is not. `_from_parts` loses
three parses and their failure modes.

    before   PathInfo: 7 derived, 5 hatched
    after    PathInfo: 7 derived, 4 hatched (14 lines)

Three new declarations, at 2, 2 and 5 hatched lines. So the total line
count went up and every line of it is a rendering or a constructor -
which is what those files are FOR.

## Surface changes, listed rather than silent

- `info.nar_hash()` is a `Hash`, not a str. `str(...)` still gives
  `sha256:<base32>`.
- `info.ca()` is a `ContentAddress | None`. `str(...)` still gives
  `fixed:r:sha256:...`.
- `info.sigs()` is a `list[Signature]`.
- `Hash`, `ContentAddress` and `Signature` are exported from
  `cythonix` and `cythonix_bindings`.

The typechecker found every caller that had been treating these as
strings, which is what the generated stubs exist for. Four call sites
across two test files, each now asking for the fact it wanted.

## What the emitter gained, and why each was forced

**`@local`.** A wire value crosses as every accessor it has, which is
what keeps anything from listing them twice - and makes this the one
thing an accessor must be able to say for itself. `Hash` forced it
immediately: two facts, three ways to print them. The reviewer of 056
predicted this exactly ("the first non-wire convenience accessor WILL
arrive") and asked for the word to be named before it was needed; it
was named one commit before it was needed.

**`_render` through the Python object.** It spelled the C++, which
held while every accessor bound a member or a no-argument call and
stopped at the first hatched one: `nix::Hash::to_string` takes a
format and a flag, so the emitted `self.to_string()` did not compile.
Going through the object also means `__str__` renders whatever
`to_string` is BOUND as - one definition rather than two that agree.

**`_from_parts = cls` is checked.** Naming the class claims the
constructor takes the wire fields in order. Nothing checked it, and it
is exactly what a forgotten `@local` breaks. It surfaced as
`__init__(): incompatible function arguments` out of a test; it is a
sentence out of the build now, naming both counts and both ways out.
Perturbation-verified.

**A wire field is checked against `SCALAR_NAMES`.** It was
`_PRIMITIVES.values()`, which maps a C++ spelling onto a Python name
for a SIGNATURE - so it admitted `float` and `None`, which no field
can be, and not `bytes`, which one can. Found by Hash's digest.

## Layout

`decl/content_address.py` held two vocabularies, one of them from
`nix/util/hash.hh`, and said in its own docstring that HashAlgorithm
"moves when something else needs it". Something did.

The vocabularies collect in `decl/words.py`. The split that matters is
COMPILES or does not: a vocabulary has no C++ behind it and no
extension to live in, so one-header-one-file - the rule for bindings -
was never the rule for these, and a file named after either header is
the wrong home for the other's words.

## Not decided

Whether `Hash` should carry the FORMAT it was parsed from.
`Hash::parseAnyReturningFormat` exists, so a caller who read
`sha256-AbCd` (SRI) and printed it back gets `sha256:1abc` (Nix32)
instead. Nothing round-trips a rendering today, so nothing has
noticed.
